<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌊 LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 9:19PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 10:19PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (nowcast)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/surface_currents/legend.png?v=lsofs-20261004-t18z-n006-lmhofs-20261004-t18z-n006-leofs-20261004-t18z-n006-loofs-20261004-t18z-n006-hec-100412-michgan-huron-100412" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Surface-current speed (centimeters per second, one continuous gradient): <b>LOWEST 0</b> dark-blue stagnant &rarr; <b>10</b> teal &rarr; <b>20</b> yellow &rarr; <b>30</b> orange &rarr; <b>50</b> red &rarr; <b>75</b> red-violet &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100 cm/s</b> dark-purple channel jets. Same speed always shows the same color; above 100 stays dark-purple. Needle arrows RIDE the flow — each sits on a short RK2 streamline integrated through the filed U/V field, tangent to local current (gently longer when faster: 6.5 px at 0 to 9.5 px at 50+ cm/s). Now: max <b>289</b>, median <b>15.1 cm/s</b>, median flow <b>161&deg; (S)</b>. NO DATA stays transparent — never zero.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NOS Great Lakes Operational Forecast System (GLOFS: LSOFS/LMHOFS/LEOFS/LOOFS) surface-current nowcast + NOAA/GLERL experimental GLCFS-FVCOM Huron-Erie Corridor & lower St. Marys River nowcast fill<br/><a href="https://opendap.co-ops.nos.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog.html">https://opendap.co-ops.nos.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog.html</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 9:19PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 10:19PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE SURFACE CURRENTS</h2><p>How fast the surface water is moving right now (color = speed) and which way it flows (arrows ride the current along its own paths). Covers all five Great Lakes plus the connecting rivers and channels where NOAA models provide current vectors.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/surface_currents/legend.png?v=lsofs-20261004-t18z-n006-lmhofs-20261004-t18z-n006-leofs-20261004-t18z-n006-loofs-20261004-t18z-n006-hec-100412-michgan-huron-100412" width="600" alt="key"></p><p>Surface-current speed (centimeters per second, one continuous gradient): <b>LOWEST 0</b> dark-blue stagnant &rarr; <b>10</b> teal &rarr; <b>20</b> yellow &rarr; <b>30</b> orange &rarr; <b>50</b> red &rarr; <b>75</b> red-violet &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100 cm/s</b> dark-purple channel jets. Same speed always shows the same color; above 100 stays dark-purple. Needle arrows RIDE the flow — each sits on a short RK2 streamline integrated through the filed U/V field, tangent to local current (gently longer when faster: 6.5 px at 0 to 9.5 px at 50+ cm/s). Now: max <b>289</b>, median <b>15.1 cm/s</b>, median flow <b>161&deg; (S)</b>. NO DATA stays transparent — never zero.</p><p><b>Units:</b> cm/s (speed gradient; arrows ride the flow)<br/><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NOS Great Lakes Operational Forecast System (GLOFS: LSOFS/LMHOFS/LEOFS/LOOFS) surface-current nowcast + NOAA/GLERL experimental GLCFS-FVCOM Huron-Erie Corridor & lower St. Marys River nowcast fill<br/><b>Update:</b> GLOFS 6-hourly cycles + GLCFS 12-hourly, checked hourly<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://opendap.co-ops.nos.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog.html">CO-OPS THREDDS</a> + <a href="https://apps.glerl.noaa.gov/thredds/catalog/glcfs/glcfs-catalog.html">GLERL GLCFS THREDDS</a></p>]]></description>
